pope francis spoke about the ongoing  conflict in his easter sunday message  from saint peter's square he expressed  his anguish over the war and hope that  peace can prevail chris van cleave  reports from london  in clear discomfort from a lingering  knee problem pope francis slowly made  his way to the balcony of saint peter's  basilica to deliver his traditional  easter speech urging the world's leaders  to strive for peace  the 85 year old pontiff described this  as an easter of war condemning the  violence in ukraine calling it cruel and  senseless implicitly criticizing russia  a message clearly resonating with the  crowd of tens of thousands  we have seen all too much blood all too  much violence in what is being  interpreted as a reference to the threat  of nuclear war francis quoted from a  1955 manifesto by philosopher bertrand  russell and physicist albert einstein  that warns of putting an end to the  human race unless mankind renounces war  to hear this stark warning the faithful  returned for the first easter weekend  since the pandemic after two years of an  empty saint peter's square the crowd  flooded into the street beyond the  square to listen and once again see pope  francis up close  but the vatican is facing criticism for  its traditional good friday procession  at rome's coliseum which included both  the ukrainian and russian cross bearer  for cbs mornings i'm chris van cleave
 

